/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:53:<h2 id="amend">Amendments to the foundation plan's D2 list — <strong>need Christie's OK</strong></h2>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:56:<tr><td>M1</td><td>"For both projects" — meaning <em>each</em> project gets its <strong>own, separate</strong> functions guard, just as each already has its own rules guard. The projects stay fully separate. tinker-hq-apps needs a guard too only because Phase E puts the one-way <code>clayhub-link</code> functions there (they watch Membership Manager's data, which lives in tinker-hq-apps).</td><td>Build my-clay-hub's guard now. Build tinker-hq-apps' own separate copy at the start of Phase E, when the link code exists. Neither guard can ever deploy to the other project.</td><td>Christie, Sep 29.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:57:<tr><td>M2</td><td>"A read-back of each function's <strong>revision</strong>"</td><td>The CLI can't read a revision id (<code>functions:list</code> has none, K10). The guard reads back instead: the CLI succeeded; the sealed manifest; a full configuration and env-key match; the content hash present, and changed exactly when the source changed; and no "skipping" line. The <strong>revision itself</strong> is read by Christie (Cloud Run → latest ready revision name, plus the Cloud Build build id), recorded in a fixed shape that <code>--attest</code> and <code>--reconcile</code> validate (F9).</td><td>The CLI exposes no revision or generation id.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:60:<tr><td>M5</td><td>"Refuses … the wrong codebase"</td><td><strong>In plain terms:</strong> functions are grouped into "codebases" (D2 has one, <code>core</code>). The guard always deploys one named group. The backstop, the last-line check the Firebase CLI runs, can tell which group is deploying only when there's more than one. With just <code>core</code>, it can't see the difference between "deploy <code>core</code>" and "deploy all functions". The difference matters because "all functions" lets the CLI silently skip some. So the guard catches that case another way: the CLI prints "Skipping the deploy of unchanged functions" when it skips, and the guard refuses to call such a deploy verified. <strong>Nothing for Christie to do.</strong> It's recorded so the gap is visible.</td><td><code>lifecycleHooks.js</code> passes only <code>GCLOUD_PROJECT</code>, <code>PROJECT_DIR</code> and <code>RESOURCE_DIR</code>.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:61:<tr><td>M6</td><td>D-4 runs "after Phase D2"</td><td>D-4 (the scheduled vault export) <strong>must first extend this guard</strong> with scheduled functions: a real Cloud Scheduler read-back, <code>retryConfig</code>, and its own review.</td><td>F13: HTTP only.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:62:<tr><td>M7</td><td>"<code>functions-build@</code>'s roles and the deployer's <code>actAs</code> land in Phase D2" (foundation; Phase D plan C5)</td><td><code>functions-build@</code> is deleted: the CLI can't use it (K12). The deployer's <code>actAs</code> on each runtime account (<code>canary@</code>) comes from Christie's Owner role, recorded in D2-1. If Owner is ever narrowed, a Service Account User grant on each runtime account replaces it. D2-2 corrects the <code>functions-build@</code> statements in both parent plans alongside M1–M6.</td><td>Round 4: the parent plans would otherwise say something false.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:86:<tr><td>K3</td><td><code>.env*</code> become env vars; <code>configDir</code> moves them; prepare writes <code>.env.&lt;id&gt;</code> only when params resolve.</td><td>F4; F13.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:93:<tr><td>K10</td><td><code>functions:list --json</code> → <code>{"status":"success","result":[…]}</code>. Each function's id, region, platform, runtime, trigger, state, expanded <code>serviceAccount</code>, <code>ingressSettings</code>, <code>uri</code> (absent until created), labels, codebase, <code>hash</code>, and its <strong>endpoint env</strong> in plain text: <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code>, <code>GCLOUD_PROJECT</code>, <code>EVENTARC_CLOUD_EVENT_SOURCE</code>, <code>FUNCTION_TARGET</code> and <code>LOG_EXECUTION_ID</code>, plus any env already live (kept on update). There is <strong>no revision id and no IAM</strong>.</td><td>F8; never prints env values; M2, M3.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:94:<tr><td>K11</td><td>No invoker → <code>["public"]</code> → <code>allUsers</code> <em>on create</em> (on update, an absent invoker skips the IAM write). <code>'private'</code> → no IAM write on create; the binding is emptied on update. An explicit list replaces the <strong>whole</strong> policy on create. Callables are always public.</td><td>every function declares its invoker (F2).</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:96:<tr><td>K13</td><td>The first <em>event-triggered</em> deploy grants the Compute account project-wide <code>run.invoker</code> and <code>eventarc.eventReceiver</code>. A deploy that fails still enables APIs and creates service agents.</td><td>not triggered in D2 (HTTP only); F9's <code>functions_unchanged</code> is named honestly.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:136:  </ul></td><td>Round 3: a missing invoker deploys public (K11). The header lets the guard tell "our code answered" from "Google refused". <code>allUsers</code> and <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code> can't be written as invokers: the CLI's <code>formatServiceAccount</code> throws on any member without <code>@</code>. So the only public paths are the literal <code>public</code> and an absent invoker, and both are refused.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:150:    <li><strong>Checks:</strong> F13, F14, F2 and F6, then F3 again.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:176:        <li>the live <code>hash</code> <strong>equals the exact expected hash</strong>. Before the CLI call, the guard computes it with the pinned 15.22.3 CLI's own functions (its packaging file list after <code>ignore</code>, then <code>applyBackendHashToBackends</code>) over: the approved folder; the backend env <code>{FIREBASE_CONFIG, GCLOUD_PROJECT}</code>, using the <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code> body from the discovery helper's own read-only fetch; and no secrets. It records that hash in the in-flight file and the receipt. This single rule covers a first deploy, a changed source, a same-sha retry and a retry after a failed build, and a wrong-but-different hash fails it. It is never compared with "different from before". A rename or mode-only change can leave the hash unchanged (the hash covers bytes only); F3's tree and mode checks cover those. Tests: a rename; a mode-only change; an arbitrary wrong hash; a same-sha retry; a failed-build retry.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:188:  </ol></td><td>Round 3: the endpoint env has five keys; a same-sha retry keeps the same hash; the header discriminates.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:192:    <li><strong>Attempt receipt</strong> (tag = <code>attempt_id</code>): <code>cli_exit</code>, <code>backstop_seen</code>, <code>skip_line_seen</code>, <code>functions_unchanged</code> (the functions snapshot only; APIs and service agents may still have changed, K13), <code>deploy_match</code>, <code>access</code>, per-function hash, the tree id and manifest sha. <code>deploy_verified=yes</code> only when <code>cli_exit=0</code>, the backstop was seen, there's no skip line, <code>deploy_match=yes</code> and every function was refused.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:215:    <li><strong>Loud skip:</strong> it prints a named <strong>SKIPPED</strong> line and exits 0 when the codebase's <code>node_modules</code> is absent <em>or the Node major isn't 22</em>, unless <code>TINKER_FUNCTIONS_TESTS=required</code>. The functions guard sets that; D2-2's own acceptance sets it; the rules guard doesn't.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:216:    <li>D2-2 proves a rules worktree's <code>npm test</code> passes.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:221:<tr><td>F13</td><td><strong>HTTP only:</strong> only <code>httpsTrigger</code> endpoints. Each other kind is refused by name, as are params and secrets. The D-4 plan adds schedules (M6).</td><td>Christie.</td></tr>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:226:<p>Order: D2-0 → D2-1 → D2-2 → D2-3 → D2-4 → D2-5. D2-2 and D2-3 are each one branch and one PR, reviewed, then merged.</p>
