/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:565:  <li><strong>Sep 29, D2-1 step 6:</strong> ✅ Artifact Registry repository <code>gcf-artifacts</code> created: Docker, Standard, <strong>us-central1 (Iowa)</strong>, Google-managed key, immutable tags off. Cleanup mode <strong>Delete artifacts</strong>, with policies <code>keep-2-recent</code> (keep most recent versions, 2) and <code>delete-old-untagged</code> (conditional delete, untagged, older than 1d), both confirmed on the details page. <strong>Vulnerability scanning disabled</strong> (it's billed per image and needs the Container Scanning API, which is off; can be revisited deliberately). Platform logs disabled (default). Repository IAM: <strong>Artifact Registry Writer</strong> granted <strong>directly</strong> on the repository (empty Inheritance column) to <code>760301318440-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com</code>; the rest are inherited Google service agents and Owner. Christie asked why this setup wasn't needed for other apps; answered that they have no server code (browser + rules only).</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:566:  <li><strong>Sep 29, D2-1 step 7:</strong> ✅ Service account <code>canary@my-clay-hub.iam.gserviceaccount.com</code> created ("Canary function", "Runs the canary function only. No roles.", no roles, no keys). The Console had auto-filled the ID <code>canary-function</code>, and Christie corrected it to <code>canary</code> before creating (IDs are permanent). <code>functions-build@</code> deleted (M7). The Service Accounts list now holds: <code>my-clay-hub@appspot</code>, <code>canary@</code>, <code>760301318440-compute@</code>, <code>firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc@</code>. All enabled, no keys.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:567:  <li><strong>Sep 29, D2-1 step 8:</strong> ✅ <strong>D2-1 complete.</strong> IAM "View by roles" with Google-provided grants (screenshot): <strong>no Editor, no Cloud Build Service Account</strong>; Logs Writer (1) and Storage Object Viewer "function source buckets only" (1), both on the Compute account only (step 5); Owner (1) Christie; no project role for <code>canary@</code>. New since D-1: <strong>Artifact Registry Service Agent (1)</strong>, a Google agent created with the repository (expected). Unchanged: Cloud Build, Cloud Functions, Cloud Run, Cloud Scheduler, Cloud Storage for Firebase, Container Registry, Firebase Admin SDK Administrator, Firebase Rules System, Firebase Service Management and Firestore service agents; Service Account Token Creator (1) and Storage Admin (1) (firebase-adminsdk, as at D-1). <strong>Next: D2-2</strong> (the repo work on branch <code>d2-functions-backstop</code>), which includes writing M1–M7 into the foundation plan and the Phase D plan.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:568:  <li><strong>Sep 29, D2-2 built</strong> on branch <code>d2-functions-backstop</code> (<code>35f8f49</code>, then review fixes). Nothing released. <strong>O1 settled:</strong> <code>scripts/functions-discover.mjs</code>, driving the pinned 15.22.3 CLI's node delegate on Node 22 with <code>FIREBASE_FUNCTIONS_DISCOVERY_OUTPUT_PATH</code>, yields the canary's whole manifest; firebase-functions 7.4.0 writes it as <strong>JSON</strong> (valid YAML), with <code>vpc: null</code> and no secrets key. Its live <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code> fetch is left to D2-3 (it takes a config file for now). Findings while building, each now in code or tests: (1) the discovery child is spawned via <code>node_modules/.bin/firebase-functions</code>, whose <code>#!/usr/bin/env node</code> shebang takes the first node on PATH (Node 20 here), so the script puts <code>$NODE22</code>'s folder first on PATH; (2) the Functions emulator spreads its whole <code>process.env</code> into every function, so the test launcher passes an <strong>allowlist</strong> (not a denylist) with a scratch <code>HOME</code>, and a second emulator session proves a function sees no parent variable outside it, decoy tokens included; (3) the emulator's <code>/backends</code> keeps only some options, so the full option set is asserted on the discovered manifest; (4) Node 22's test runner takes globs, not folders. <strong>Deviations, logged:</strong> <code>firebase-admin@14.5.0</code> is pinned exactly beside <code>firebase-functions</code> (a required peer that npm installs anyway; pinning keeps it fixed); the backstop <em>also</em> checks the sealed manifest against <code>functions/declarations.json</code> (HTTP only, invoker never public and as declared, account, ingress, same function set) — defence in depth for K11, since the guard (F2) remains the primary check. Implementation review: Codex "merge after fixes" (1 blocking, 3 should-fix, 1 nit), Claude "merge after fixes" (0 blocking, 8 should-fix, 10 nits); full texts <code>thoughts/reviews/firebase-functions-deploy-guard-d2-2-impl-{codex,claude}.md</code>. Applied: a failure after the seal's copy leaves no <code>functions.yaml</code> (checked on a private copy, then an EXIT trap until the success line; failure-injection test); a mismatch of thousands of files no longer dies of SIGPIPE before its REFUSED line (<code>|| true</code> on each <code>head</code>, 3,000-file test); a real two-codebase CLI-path test for M5's <code>RESOURCE_DIR</code> refusal; the Node-22 refusal tested on the host Node; discovery refuses without the codebase's own SDK install and prints its version; the launcher's allowlist; <code>functions-emulator.mjs</code> and <code>functions-discover.mjs</code> added to the rules guard's <code>CONTROL_FILES</code> (<strong>D2-3's control-file list must include them too</strong>); hub port pinned away from 4400; a symlink at the seal path refused; a committed quoted file name tested; <code>AGENTS.md</code>, the Phase D plan's D-1 step 11 and the package.json em dash fixed. <strong>Carried into D2-3 as requirements</strong> (the backstop can't enforce them): (a) a fresh, private worktree for every run, retries included — the backstop proves the folder when it runs, not afterwards, and refuses to seal over an existing <code>functions.yaml</code>; (b) the guard deletes the seal on cleanup. Documented as honest limits in the backstop's header: a symlinked top-level <code>node_modules</code> is skipped (the <code>ignore</code> entry keeps it out of the upload); only <code>jq</code> is pinned. Not changed: the sealed-manifest-inside-the-codebase check stays although F3 makes it unreachable.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:569:  <li><strong>Sep 29, D2-2 review round 2</strong> (Christie chose a confirming pass before merging): Codex on the fix commit <code>e6044f9</code> only, "merge after fixes" (1 blocking, 1 should-fix); text <code>thoughts/reviews/firebase-functions-deploy-guard-d2-2-impl-codex-round2.md</code>. It confirmed every earlier finding fixed or correctly deferred, and the jq declarations check correct. Applied in <code>f44d912</code>: (1) the EXIT trap saves <code>$?</code> and removes the seal on <em>any</em> non-zero exit, including a failure to write the success line after <code>sealed=1</code>, with each cleanup step independent (test with stdout closed; the old trap fails it); (2) discovery requires <code>.bin/firebase-functions</code> to resolve into the codebase's own <code>firebase-functions</code> package, and reads the printed version from there (test: a redirected binary refuses). Tests: 193 unit + 178 guard + 7 functions + 2 env probe; the rules suite had to wait for port 8180 — another session's Classbook e2e emulator (<code>demo-tinker-classbook</code>) held it; left alone. It then passed 194/194.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:570:  <li><strong>Sep 29: ✅ D2-2 complete.</strong> Merged to <code>main</code> by fast-forward to <code>f44d912</code> (PR Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub#2), with Christie's go-ahead. Nothing released. <strong>Next: D2-3</strong> on branch <code>d2-functions-guard</code> — <code>scripts/deploy-functions.sh</code>, <code>scripts/lib/receipts.sh</code>, the live <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code> fetch in <code>functions-discover.mjs</code>; its control-file list includes <code>functions-emulator.mjs</code> and <code>functions-discover.mjs</code>; a fresh private worktree per run, retries included; the seal deleted on cleanup; <code>npm test</code> gains <code>test:functions-guard</code> (update the <code>npm test</code> string asserted in <code>tests/unit/functions-emulator.test.js</code>).</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:571:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-3 decision (Christie):</strong> <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code> is <strong>pinned</strong> in <code>functions/firebase-config.json</code> (a control file, shown in <code>--diff</code>), not fetched. The pinned 15.22.3 CLI has no command that prints the adminSdkConfig, so a live fetch would have meant a script loading the CLI's code and calling Google with Christie's cached login — against her credential rule. Consequences: the pin's key order is part of the hash; its <code>locationId</code> ("nam5") is unconfirmed until the first deploy; F8 also compares the live <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code> and <code>GCLOUD_PROJECT</code> values, and on a mismatch the guard prints the live <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code> (the non-secret admin-SDK config — the one exception to "never print env values"). A wrong pin can't be fixed by <code>--reverify</code> (it rebuilds from the attempt's commit, pin included): it means a corrected commit and a second deploy under a new approval (FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md §8). This changes D2-5 step 2 for that one case.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:572:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-3 built</strong> on branch <code>d2-functions-guard</code> (<code>c78d9a1</code>, review fixes <code>688e66f</code>). Nothing released. Files: <code>scripts/deploy-functions.sh</code> (all seven modes); <code>scripts/lib/receipts.sh</code> (the rules guard's record block, extracted unchanged — its suite passes, 196 — plus fail-closed readers <code>record_field</code>, <code>records_for</code>, <code>deploy_verified_of</code>, <code>iam_attested_of</code>); <code>scripts/lib/functions-hash.mjs</code> (the expected hash with the CLI's own <code>prepareFunctionsUpload</code>, <code>resolveBackend</code>, <code>applyBackendHashToBackends</code>; pinned by an independent K17 recompute in <code>tests/unit/functions-hash.test.js</code>); <code>functions-discover.mjs</code> prints <code>firebaseConfigEnv</code>, <code>sourceHash</code>, <code>expectedHashes</code>; <code>scripts/deploy-functions.test.sh</code> (534 checks, fakes for Node 22, npm, the CLI, discovery and curl; the real backstop and library), wired in as <code>test:functions-guard</code>. Deviations and details, logged: (1) <code>predeploy-check.sh</code> gains a check-only target <code>functions-folder</code> for F3 before and after discovery (the CLI can't run it: F4 pins the hook to <code>functions</code>); (2) discovery runs under <code>env -i</code> with a scratch HOME; (3) every tag-writing mode refuses unless the running guard and library are byte-equal to <code>origin/main</code>'s; a changed verifier (any CONTROL_FILE differing between the attempt and the tip) refuses until Christie OKs it, then <code>--acknowledge-verifier-change</code> records <code>verifier_changed=yes</code> (F9's "acknowledges before the record counts"); (4) an attempt's <code>deploy_verified</code> is its newest verify record's, else its own; (5) the in-flight file gains <code>cli_exit</code> once the CLI returns, so a crash during the read-back can still verify through <code>--reconcile</code>; transcripts persist in <code>.git/tinker-deploy-transcripts/</code>; (6) <code>--reconcile</code> evidence needs a finished build and a revision; otherwise <code>--clear-inflight</code>; (7) <code>--attest</code> records content mismatches as <code>iam_attested=no</code> and refuses only a malformed shape; (8) the manifest check allowlists endpoint keys and refuses requiredAPIs, extensions and manifest env; (9) <code>--approved</code> takes any commit on <code>origin/main</code> whose control files equal the tip's, as the rules guard does (FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md §1's "only the tip" corrected); (10) codebase names ending <code>-verify</code>/<code>-attest</code> are refused; (11) npm pinned at exactly 10.9.9; (12) the rules guard's CONTROL_FILES gain the library, the hash module, the functions guard and its suite (npm test runs them), and its harness has a stand-in for every control file (D2-2's lacked two). Mutation check: three deliberately broken guards (probe ignored, drift check removed, stale clock allowed) each failed the suite. Real-repo smoke test: <code>--status</code>/<code>--diff</code> read correctly; <code>--approved</code>'s preflight passed on the real Node 22, npm and CLI, then refused as unpushed. Full <code>npm test</code> with <code>TINKER_FUNCTIONS_TESTS=required</code> green at <code>c78d9a1</code> (202 unit, 196 guard, 507 functions guard, 9 + 2 functions, 194 rules).</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:573:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-3 implementation review:</strong> Claude "merge after fixes" (1 blocking, 5 should-fix, 4 nits; text <code>thoughts/reviews/firebase-functions-deploy-guard-d2-3-impl-claude.md</code>). It verified in source the expected hash, F8's field names and the five env keys, the public-path closure, and that the receipts extraction is behaviour-identical. Codex <strong>did not finish</strong>: the workspace ran out of usage partway through. Applied in <code>688e66f</code>: <code>iam-expectations.json</code> now expects <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.builder</code> to have no holders and <code>logging.logWriter</code> / <code>storage.objectViewer</code> on the Compute account only, so <code>--attest</code> actually checks F7 (blocking); a declared runtime account missing from <code>iam-expectations.json</code> is a mismatch; <code>--diff</code> and <code>--approved</code> name every changed machinery file with a stat; <code>functions:list</code> failures keep the CLI's own <code>--json</code> error (it's on stdout); RULES-ROLLBACK.md says npm test now takes about 10 minutes under the lock; usage shows the acknowledge flag; <code>atomic_write</code> cleans up. Not changed: the library is sourced before the tip check (its own <code>fetch_state</code> is needed to find the tip; an edited library is still refused before anything is written or deployed). Suite after fixes: 534 passed. Next: Christie decides whether to wait for a Codex pass, then merge (fast-forward, PR), then D2-4.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:574:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-3 Codex review</strong> (Christie reset Codex; on <code>688e66f</code>): "not ready" (1 blocking, 3 should-fix; text <code>thoughts/reviews/firebase-functions-deploy-guard-d2-3-impl-codex.md</code>). It independently confirmed the expected hash, F8's field names, that the pinned <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code> fails closed, the <code>cli_exit</code> extension, and the receipts extraction, and found no route to a wrong <code>deploy_verified=yes</code>. Applied: (blocking) <code>--attest</code> could write <code>iam_attested=yes</code> without proving F7 — the build roles were compared with a fixed account, not the one that built, and the Storage Object Viewer condition and the <code>gcf-artifacts</code> Artifact Registry Writer weren't read. Now <code>iam-expectations.json</code> names <code>buildCandidates</code>, <code>buildAccountRoles</code>, <code>buildRoleConditions</code> (A5, compared ignoring whitespace) and <code>artifactRegistryWriterOn</code>; the build roles and the repository writer must be held by the reported build account and nobody else, so §3's move to the legacy account attests without an edit. The printed <code>--attest</code> template is generated from that file. Deviation, logged: rebuilding an attempt's expected state (<code>--reconcile</code>, <code>--reverify</code>) deliberately uses the attempt's own backstop, discovery and hash module — they produced what it recorded, and their output must equal it — while every verdict is the tip's; records name <code>rebuild_helpers_from</code>, and a changed helper still needs Christie's acknowledgement. Tests added for each case, plus the untested manifest and declaration branches. Suite: 580 passed.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:575:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-3 Codex confirming passes</strong> (Christie chose them before merging). Round 2, on the fixes (<code>75e8f09</code>): "not ready" (1 blocking, 1 should-fix; text <code>…-d2-3-impl-codex-round2.md</code>). It confirmed <code>rebuild_helpers_from</code> is sound and every earlier finding fixed or logged, but the IAM reading still reduced bindings to holder sets: an extra, unconditional <code>storage.objectViewer</code> binding of the build account (which overrides the A5-conditioned one) or a project-level <code>artifactregistry.writer</code> grant could pass; and <code>--diff</code> showed no machinery on the first deploy. Fixed in <code>423c004</code>: the build roles are read as binding rows (member and condition), exactly one per role, to the account that built, with A5's condition (Logs Writer: none); Artifact Registry Writer is read at project level (expected none) and directly on <code>gcf-artifacts</code>; the first deploy's diff lists the machinery as "all new to the record". Round 3, on <code>423c004</code>: all three confirmed fixed, no new bug; it raised grants of <code>storage.objectViewer</code> on individual buckets or managed folders, which the IAM project page doesn't show. <strong>Christie, Sep 30: a known limit, not read.</strong> The attest record (<code>not_attested=</code>), the printed instructions and FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md §7 say so (<code>8eb9f5a</code>). Full <code>npm test</code> with the functions tests required green on <code>423c004</code> (202 unit, 196 guard, 595 functions guard, 9 + 2 functions, 194 rules); the functions guard suite 596 on <code>8eb9f5a</code>. Texts: <code>thoughts/reviews/firebase-functions-deploy-guard-d2-3-impl-codex-round{2,3}.md</code>.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:576:  <li><strong>Sep 30: ✅ D2-3 complete.</strong> Merged to <code>main</code> by fast-forward to <code>8eb9f5a</code> (PR Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub#3), after Christie chose the Codex confirming passes. Nothing released. <strong>Next: D2-4</strong> — Appendix A1–A4 (the hook gains F12's function-changing commands; global and repo CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md; settings), applied as in D-3: backups, fixtures across F12's matrix, valid JSON, a diff equal to the approved hunks, a fresh-session check, then delete the backups. Carry into D2-5: the pinned <code>FIREBASE_CONFIG</code>'s <code>locationId</code> is unconfirmed (a mismatch means a corrected commit and a second deploy, not a <code>--reverify</code>); Christie's <code>--attest</code> reading now includes each build-role binding row with its condition, <code>artifactregistry.writer</code> at project level and on <code>gcf-artifacts</code>, and the build account; the guard prints the exact JSON shape.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:577:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-4 applied (steps before the fresh-session check).</strong> Christie OK'd the exact A2/A4 text drafted from Appendix A's bullets ("apply as shown"). (1) A3, repo docs first: <code>~/my-clay-hub/CLAUDE.md</code> and <code>AGENTS.md</code> (<code>4f0b5cd</code>, PR Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub#4, fast-forwarded). (2) Backups in <code>~/.claude/backups-d2-4-2026-09-30/</code>. (3) A1, the hook (tinker-ai-configs <code>a53893e</code>): the F12 commands added under the same token rules, and A1's message word for word; a permanent matrix, <code>scripts/firebase-deploy-hook.test.sh</code>, 68 cases (every raw-deploy form, every F12 command in three spellings, allowed read-only siblings, both known gaps), written with the Write tool. Deviation, logged: the subcommand now also ends at <code>; &amp; | )</code> or a backtick — before, only whitespace or the end did, so <code>firebase deploy;ls</code> and <code>$(firebase deploy)</code> slipped through although the hook's comment claimed <code>$(…)</code> was caught. Known gaps, documented and tested: a backslash-newline split, and a subcommand directly followed by a quote (<code>bash -c "firebase deploy"</code>; the same rule that lets a quoted mention through). The live hook denied one of this session's own edit commands with the new message — proof it's wired. (4) A4, <code>settings.json</code>: the soft_deny approval entry names the functions guard and says "only for that sha and that guard"; the hard_deny raw-deploy entry names it and adds the F12 commands; the environment guards entry names it. JSON valid; the PreToolUse hook entry intact (<code>… || exit 2</code>); entry counts unchanged (4/4/6); the diff is exactly those three entries. (5) A2, <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>: the functions guard in my-clay-hub's Deploy guard cell, a ✅ row, and the ❌ row gains the F12 commands (plus <code>functions:secrets:access</code>, which the hook also denies); the diff against the backup is exactly those three hunks. <strong>Next:</strong> the check in a FRESH session (the wording binds only sessions started after it), then delete the backups; then D2-5.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:578:  <li><strong>Sep 30: ✅ D2-4 complete (fresh-session check).</strong> A session started after the change confirmed: (1) the loaded <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> shows the functions guard in my-clay-hub's Deploy guard cell, the ✅ row and the ❌ row with the F12 commands; (2) <code>firebase-deploy-hook.test.sh</code> 68 passed, and a live Bash command whose text held the function-delete command was denied with the message naming all three guards; (3) <code>deploy-rules.sh --status</code> works. <strong>Found:</strong> <code>deploy-functions.sh --status</code> run by path failed with "permission denied" — the guard was committed <code>100644</code> (its suite runs it through <code>bash</code>, so nothing noticed; the <code>--approved</code> line it prints would have failed at D2-5). Fixed in <code>6d58838</code> (PR Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub#5, rebased onto main with Christie's OK): mode change only (blob <code>5b3764d</code> unchanged) plus <code>tests/unit/guard-modes.test.js</code>, which requires both guards run by path to be committed <code>100755</code> (<code>predeploy-check.sh</code> stays <code>100644</code>; it is only run as <code>bash …</code>). Full suite with <code>TINKER_FUNCTIONS_TESTS=required</code>: unit 203, rules guard 196, functions guard 596, functions 9 + 2, rules 194 — all passed. The backups folder was moved to the macOS Trash (not hard-deleted); Christie empties it. <strong>Next: D2-5</strong> — Christie's IAM "View by roles" check first (neither build candidate holds Editor or Cloud Build Service Account), then <code>--status</code>, <code>--diff</code> verbatim, the phrase for the then-current <code>origin/main</code> sha, <code>--approved</code>, then her <code>--attest</code> reading.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:579:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-5 pre-ask IAM re-check:</strong> ✅ Christie's IAM "View by roles" screenshot (Google-provided grants shown): <strong>no Editor, no Cloud Build Service Account</strong>. Rows identical to D2-1 step 8: Artifact Registry, Cloud Build, Cloud Functions, Cloud Run, Cloud Scheduler, Cloud Storage for Firebase, Container Registry, Firebase Admin SDK Administrator, Firebase Service Management and Firestore service agents, Firebase Rules System (1 each); Logs Writer (1); Owner (1); Service Account Token Creator (1); Storage Admin (1); Storage Object Viewer "function source buckets only" (1). <code>--status</code>: no attempt yet for core; origin/main <code>6d58838e2c6b2aa111cbb5b1596a9e78bfcbaf9a</code>. <code>--diff</code>: 3,564 lines, first deploy so everything is new (40 machinery files by stat, 18,722 insertions, most of it the two lockfiles); every hunk is from the reviewed D-2/D2-2/D2-3/D2-4 commits.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:580:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-5 first <code>--approved</code> run: REFUSED, nothing deployed.</strong> Christie said "approved to change firebase 6d58838e2c6b2aa111cbb5b1596a9e78bfcbaf9a". The guard installed the codebase, the folder check matched (tree <code>42aebde0</code>), discovery gave manifest <code>10007898…</code> (canary), then <code>npm test</code> in its worktree failed at once: "REFUSED: tests failed on 6d58838e2c6b; nothing was deployed." (exit 15; no attempt recorded — <code>--status</code> still "none"). <strong>Cause:</strong> the guard runs the suite with Node 22 first on PATH, and Node 22 reads <code>node --test tests/unit/ tests/shared/</code> as module paths ("Cannot find module …/tests/shared"); Node 20, the host default under which every earlier full run passed, searched the folders. The guard's own suite fakes npm, so nothing ran the real suite under Node 22. Same pattern in <code>test:rules</code> (<code>tests/rules/</code>) and in <code>tz-matrix.test.js</code> (spawned with <code>process.execPath</code>). The guard did its job: it refused on red tests. <strong>Fix</strong> on branch <code>fix/node22-test-paths</code>: the npm scripts name <code>*.test.js</code> files (shell glob), tz-matrix lists the shared test files, and new <code>tests/unit/test-scripts.test.js</code> fails if any <code>node --test</code> script passes a folder (fails on the old <code>package.json</code>, passes on the new). The approval for <code>6d58838</code> is spent: the fix is a new commit and needs Christie's phrase for its own sha.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:581:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-5 fix reviewed and tested.</strong> <code>fa76d36</code>, then Codex (Christie: "yes") "ready after fixes" — 0 blocking; 2 should-fix applied in <code>d16e23a</code> (the folder check also catches a folder without a trailing slash or quoted; every file Node's own search would run under a searched folder must be a flat <code>*.test.js</code>); nit left (<code>MIN_SHARED_TESTS</code> is a floor, completeness is now checked separately). Text: <code>thoughts/reviews/firebase-functions-deploy-guard-d2-5-node22-codex.md</code>. Full suite on <code>fa76d36</code>, Node 22 first on PATH as the guard runs it: unit 203 + 1 intentional skip, rules guard 196, functions guard 596, functions 9 + 2, rules 194; Node 20: 204 / 196 / 596 / 9 + 2 / 194. Unit on <code>d16e23a</code>: 204 + 1 skip (22), 205 (20). PR Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub#6. <strong>Next:</strong> Christie's OK to merge, then <code>--status</code>, <code>--diff</code>, her phrase for the new sha, <code>--approved</code>.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:582:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-5 second <code>--approved</code> run (on <code>604844c469251121e3cb3c2f2c7a92969f5bfea0</code>, PR #6 merged with Christie's OK; tree identical to the reviewed <code>d16e23a</code>; Christie said the phrase for it after the new <code>--diff</code>): REFUSED, nothing deployed.</strong> The full suite passed in the worktree under Node 22; the pre-deploy <code>functions:list</code> then failed (exit 13, no attempt recorded). Diagnosed read-only (<code>functions:list --debug</code>, <code>login:list</code>): Google returned 403 on <code>testIamPermissions</code> and on the v1 functions list because the Firebase CLI was signed in as Christie's <strong>personal</strong> account (<code>christieshubley@gmail.com</code>, from the day's Hubley Hub work), which has no access to <code>my-clay-hub</code>. The guard failed closed as designed. Christie re-signed the CLI: default <code>Christie@tinkerartstudio.com</code>, personal account kept as a second one. <code>functions:list</code> then returned <code>{"status":"success","result":[]}</code>. Lesson: the guard runs the CLI from a temp worktree, so it always uses the CLI's <em>default</em> account; a per-folder <code>login:use</code> doesn't reach it. Third run started under the same approval (same sha; nothing had reached Google).</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:583:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-5 third run — the canary is DEPLOYED, recorded <code>deploy_verified=no</code></strong> (exit 21). Attempt <code>deployed/my-clay-hub/functions-core/20261001T034143Z-604844c</code>. Suite green in the worktree (Node 22); the CLI enabled the needed APIs (cloudfunctions, cloudbuild, artifactregistry, run, eventarc, pubsub, storage were already on; it <strong>enabled firebaseextensions.googleapis.com</strong> and generated the pubsub and eventarc service identities), uploaded 35.62 KB, "Successful create operation" for <code>core:canary(us-central1)</code>, URL <code>https://us-central1-my-clay-hub.cloudfunctions.net/canary</code>. <code>cli_exit=0 backstop_seen=yes skip_line_seen=no</code>. Probe: <strong>refused http-403 Google_Frontend</strong> (private, as declared). Read-back <code>deploy_match: no</code>: the live FIREBASE_CONFIG is <code>{"projectId":"my-clay-hub","storageBucket":"my-clay-hub.firebasestorage.app"}</code> — <strong>no locationId</strong> — so the pinned "nam5" was wrong; live hash <code>5dc5ef93…</code>, expected <code>ef4fe177…</code>. As planned (§8): a corrected commit and a second deploy. Fix on branch <code>fix/firebase-config-pin</code> (<code>3ece046</code>): the pin is the live value byte for byte; with it the guard's discovery computes <code>5dc5ef9345d6bdc235abf23407c4815ce8405a3a</code>, equal to the live hash. Checked in the pinned CLI: <code>--only functions:core</code> sets <code>targetedByOnly</code> (codebase filter matches), so <code>toSkipPredicate</code> is false and the second deploy updates rather than skips. Codex review and the full suite running. Attest waits for the second deploy (only the newest attempt can be attested).</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:584:  <li><strong>Sep 30, D2-5 pin fix merged and redeployed — ✅ <code>deploy_verified=yes</code>.</strong> Codex on <code>3ece046</code>: "ready", no findings (text <code>thoughts/reviews/firebase-functions-deploy-guard-d2-5-pin-codex.md</code>). Full suite under Node 22 green. PR Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub#7 merged with Christie's OK (main <code>7efa0f06d0bc638c8cdc0037bc30913e5ea84a0f</code>, tree identical to the reviewed commit). New <code>--diff</code> differed from <code>604844c</code>'s only by the pin, the runbook and the new test, plus the "newest attempt is NOT verified" note. Christie said "approved to change firebase 7efa0f06d0bc638c8cdc0037bc30913e5ea84a0f". The guard: suite green, backstop seen, "Successful <strong>update</strong> operation" for <code>core:canary(us-central1)</code> (no skip line, as predicted), <code>deploy_match: yes</code>, probe <strong>refused http-403 Google_Frontend</strong>, record <code>deployed/my-clay-hub/functions-core/20261001T045900Z-7efa0f0</code>. <strong>Next:</strong> Christie's IAM reading, then <code>--attest</code>.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:585:  <li><strong>Oct 1, D2-5 attest — recorded <code>iam_attested=no</code> (one difference, understood).</strong> Christie's Console reading (screenshots): build <code>6d393ad7-ba48-4326-b27f-109756ae0a61</code> SUCCESS, built by <code>760301318440-compute@</code>; Cloud Run <code>canary</code> latest ready revision <code>canary-00002-huv</code> (100%; <code>-00001-det</code> 0%), Require authentication on (IAM), runs as <code>canary@</code>, no Cloud Run Invoker on the service; IAM View by roles (Google-provided grants shown): Owner = Christie; Logs Writer = Compute, no condition; Storage Object Viewer = Compute with the A5 condition (pasted JSON character-identical to <code>iam-expectations.json</code>); no run.invoker, cloudfunctions.invoker, cloudbuild.builds.builder or project-level artifactregistry.writer; <code>canary@</code> has no project roles; <code>gcf-artifacts</code> Artifact Registry Writer = Compute only, direct. <strong>New since the pre-ask reading: Editor (1) = <code>760301318440@cloudservices.gserviceaccount.com</code></strong> (Google APIs Service Agent, Google-managed), plus a Firebase Extensions API Service Agent row — both most likely from the CLI enabling <code>firebaseextensions.googleapis.com</code> during the first deploy (not proven which API triggered the Editor grant). It is not a build candidate and nothing we deploy runs as it; Google advises against removing its Editor grant, and the CLI re-enables the Extensions API on every functions deploy. Christie: <strong>don't remove it; record the honest reading</strong>. <code>--attest</code> → record <code>deployed/my-clay-hub/functions-core-attest/20261001T141405Z-7efa0f0</code>, <code>iam_attested=no</code>, sole reason "roles/editor: holders [serviceaccount:760301318440@cloudservices.gserviceaccount.com], expected []". <code>--status</code>: verified at <code>…/20261001T045900Z-7efa0f0</code>, <code>iam_attested=no</code>. <strong>Open decisions for Christie (before the next functions deploy):</strong> (1) whether <code>iam-expectations.json</code> explicitly allows exactly this Google-managed agent as an Editor holder (still refusing Editor for anything else), reviewed, then re-attest; (2) make attest lighter — Christie asked (Oct 1) why so much manual checking; options: re-read IAM only when functions, accounts or <code>iam-expectations.json</code> change, or give the guard a narrow read-only way to read IAM itself (a credentials decision). D2's deploy work is otherwise done.</li>
/Users/christiehubley/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/firebase-functions-deploy-guard.html:586:  <li><strong>Oct 1, both decisions: Christie said yes to both</strong> (DECISIONS #60–61). Built on branch <code>d2-attest-followups</code>: (1) <code>iam-expectations.json</code> allows exactly the Google APIs Service Agent as Editor; (2) the guard records <code>attest_needed</code> + why on every attempt record (<code>--approved</code>, <code>--reconcile</code>, <code>--clear-inflight</code>), shows it in <code>--status</code>, and prints the reading's shape only when needed (fail-closed; a reading that came back no, or was needed and never got a yes, stays owed). Codex round 1 "not ready" (3 blocking, 1 should-fix) → <code>c562f69</code>; round 2 "ready after fixes" (2 should-fix) → <code>cabae43</code>. Full suite on <code>cabae43</code> under Node 22 green (functions guard 636). PR Tinker-Art-Studio/my-clay-hub#8. <strong>Correction:</strong> <code>--attest</code> judges a reading by the ATTEMPT's own <code>iam-expectations.json</code>, so <code>7efa0f0</code> stays <code>iam_attested=no</code>; no redeploy just to flip it — the next functions deploy needs a full reading under the new file. <strong>Paused here (Christie moving locations). Next:</strong> Christie's OK to merge PR #8; then Phase E prerequisites (the post-D2 vault export, then review the Phase E plan).</li>
