53:    <li>Sep 27: every product decision answered by Christie. Two facts are read at setup time: the project ID Google accepts (D12) and <code>tinker-hq-apps</code>' Firestore location (D23). Reviews: round 1 (Codex + Claude), then round 2 (Codex + Claude, the same day). This version is the round-2 rewrite: all findings addressed, superseded options removed. See <a href="#reviews">Reviews</a>.</li>
67:  Membership Manager ──writes──▶ clayHub_members                     ingestMemberUpdate   ◀── the ONLY way in (private HTTPS)
69:  Manage Team ─────────────────▶ users                                  writes members/* and staffRoster/* only · never deletes
71:                                   │  clayhub-link (2nd gen)          staffRoster/staff_{uid}     explicitly granted staff only
77:  <li><strong>The link never forwards an event as it arrives.</strong> When anything changes, it <em>re-reads the current truth</em> for that member from <code>tinker-hq-apps</code> and sends a full snapshot stamped with the time it read it (D22). A later snapshot always wins. That makes deletes, email changes and events arriving out of order all safe.</li>
134:  <strong>Dependency:</strong> <code>staffRoster</code> is built from <code>users</code>. So the self-create pins in the <code>users</code> rules (<code>firestore.rules:127-145</code>: <code>role:'staff'</code>, empty <code>appAccess</code>, both unchanged on self-update) become a My Clay Hub security control. E-0 records this, and the link only projects <em>explicit</em> grants.
169:<tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:24%">Decision</th><th>Answer</th></tr>
174:    <li>looks up <code>staffRoster/staff_{uid}</code>, which must exist with an explicit grant;</li>
179:    <li><strong>Staff rules check <code>staffRoster/{request.auth.uid}</code> live on every request</strong>, so unticking the box in Manage Team takes effect within a minute, through the link.</li>
191:<tr><td>D4</td><td>What decides a member's status</td><td>A single tested function, <code>deriveStatus(source, todayDenver)</code>. It uses Denver calendar dates and parses <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> strings as dates, never through <code>new Date()</code>. It runs <strong>inside <code>ingestMemberUpdate</code></strong> on every write (Phase E), and again for everyone at <strong>3:30 AM Denver</strong> daily (Phase E; this avoids the DST gap). Rules are applied in order; the first match wins:
208:<tr><td>D7</td><td>Collection names</td><td>Plain names in <code>my-clay-hub</code>: <code>members</code>, <code>staffRoster</code>, <code>bookings</code>, <code>activityLog</code>, <code>inbox</code>, <code>settings</code>, ….</td></tr>
212:<tr><td>D11</td><td>Permissions inventory (copied into the resilience plan's IAM inventory)</td><td>See <a href="#iam">the IAM inventory</a> below. Every grant runs one way, uses dedicated service accounts, and needs no key files.</td></tr>
248:<tr><td>D22</td><td>How the link delivers changes</td><td>Approved (Christie, Sep 27). <strong>"Re-read, then send a snapshot."</strong>
259:    <li>It POSTs <code>{memberId, snapshot, readTime}</code> to <code>ingestMemberUpdate</code>.</li>
262:    <li><strong>The reconcile uses the same envelope and the same gate.</strong> It reads the whole source in <strong>one read-only transaction pinned to a single <code>readTime</code></strong>, and stamps every member (and tombstone) with that time. So a trigger snapshot taken during a reconcile can never be overwritten by older reconcile data.</li>
264:  Because every message is the whole truth as of its read time, arrival order, retries, deletes and email changes (the new doc and the retired old doc in one batch) all end in the right state. <code>users/{uid}</code> works the same way, into <code>staffRoster/staff_{uid}</code>, using that document read's <code>DocumentSnapshot.readTime</code>. There's no <code>memberId</code> and no conflict case on that path. Docs without a <code>memberId</code> are skipped with an alert (Phase E requires Phase B). <strong>Phone (keeps SPEC DECISIONS #24):</strong> the snapshot carries only <code>phoneLast4</code>, worked out in <code>tinker-hq-apps</code>. The full phone number never leaves that project. No client can read <code>members/*</code> at all. Members read only their own <code>memberProfiles/{memberId}</code>, an allowlist without <code>phoneLast4</code>, also written by <code>ingestMemberUpdate</code> (Phase C verification finding). Only the kiosk lookup function (<code>kioskLookup</code>) uses <code>phoneLast4</code>, and it returns just the first name and last initial, plus booking status.</td></tr>
265:<tr><td>D23</td><td>Functions generation and locations</td><td><strong>2nd gen everywhere.</strong> Each project records <em>three</em> separate values:
