   241	<tr><td>D20</td><td>Brand</td><td>See <a href="#brand">Brand</a>.</td></tr>
   242	<tr><td>D21</td><td>Permanent member ID</td><td>Approved (Christie, Sep 27). Built in Phase B, which is on hold until her other Membership Manager session is merged.
   243	  <ul>
   244	    <li>A random, never-changing <code>memberId</code> on every record. It's backfilled once (after a snapshot), never overwritten, and carried through every save and the Sawyer import.</li>
   245	    <li><strong>"Change email"</strong> is one atomic batch: create the new doc (same <code>memberId</code>, live) and mark the old doc <code>retired:true</code>. It refuses if a live doc already exists for the new email.</li>
   246	    <li>Doc IDs stay as they are.</li>
   247	  </ul></td></tr>
   248	<tr><td>D22</td><td>How the link delivers changes</td><td>Approved (Christie, Sep 27). <strong>"Re-read, then send a snapshot."</strong>
   249	  <ul>
   250	    <li>A trigger on <code>clayHub_members/{id}</code> collects the <code>memberId</code> from both the before and after versions.</li>
   251	    <li>For each one, it reads <em>all</em> source docs with that <code>memberId</code> in a single query, filtering <strong>on <code>memberId</code> only</strong>. "Live" is decided in memory as <code>retired !== true</code>, because live docs usually have no <code>retired</code> field. The query's <code>QuerySnapshot.readTime</code> is the snapshot time.</li>
   252	    <li>It works out the member's current truth:
   253	      <ul>
   254	        <li>exactly one live doc → that doc's fields;</li>
   255	        <li>no live doc → a tombstone;</li>
   256	        <li><strong>more than one live doc → a conflict:</strong> nothing is sent, and it alerts.</li>
   257	      </ul>
   258	    </li>
   259	    <li>It POSTs <code>{memberId, snapshot, readTime}</code> to <code>ingestMemberUpdate</code>.</li>
   260	    <li>That function, in a transaction, <strong>drops anything whose <code>readTime</code> isn't later than the stored <code>sourceReadTime</code></strong>. A member with no stored time accepts any snapshot. It checks the payload strictly, runs <code>deriveStatus</code>, and writes. It never deletes.</li>
   261	    <li><code>sourceReadTime</code> is a <code>tinker-hq-apps</code> timestamp, kept in its own field. It's only ever compared with other <code>tinker-hq-apps</code> read times, never with <code>my-clay-hub</code> clocks or <code>updatedAt</code>.</li>
   262	    <li><strong>The reconcile uses the same envelope and the same gate.</strong> It reads the whole source in <strong>one read-only transaction pinned to a single <code>readTime</code></strong>, and stamps every member (and tombstone) with that time. So a trigger snapshot taken during a reconcile can never be overwritten by older reconcile data.</li>
   263	  </ul>
   264	  Because every message is the whole truth as of its read time, arrival order, retries, deletes and email changes (the new doc and the retired old doc in one batch) all end in the right state. <code>users/{uid}</code> works the same way, into <code>staffRoster/staff_{uid}</code>, using that document read's <code>DocumentSnapshot.readTime</code>. There's no <code>memberId</code> and no conflict case on that path. Docs without a <code>memberId</code> are skipped with an alert (Phase E requires Phase B). <strong>Phone (keeps SPEC DECISIONS #24):</strong> the snapshot carries only <code>phoneLast4</code>, worked out in <code>tinker-hq-apps</code>. The full phone number never leaves that project. No client can read <code>members/*</code> at all. Members read only their own <code>memberProfiles/{memberId}</code>, an allowlist without <code>phoneLast4</code>, also written by <code>ingestMemberUpdate</code> (Phase C verification finding). Only the kiosk lookup function (<code>kioskLookup</code>) uses <code>phoneLast4</code>, and it returns just the first name and last initial, plus booking status.</td></tr>
   265	<tr><td>D23</td><td>Functions generation and locations</td><td><strong>2nd gen everywhere.</strong> Each project records <em>three</em> separate values:
   266	  <ul>
   267	    <li>(1) the Firestore location (Christie reads <code>tinker-hq-apps</code>' in the Console; <code>my-clay-hub</code> matches it, permanently);</li>
   268	    <li>(2) the Eventarc trigger location, which must equal the database location;</li>
   269	    <li>(3) the function region. For a <code>nam5</code> multi-region database, that's <code>us-central1</code>.</li>
   270	  </ul>
   271	  Also: explicit <code>maxInstances</code>, the Admin app built once per instance, an Artifact Registry cleanup policy, and <strong>retries set explicitly</strong> (<code>retry: true</code> on the triggers, <code>retryConfig</code> on the schedules; both are off by default).</td></tr>
   272	</table>
