   274	<h3 id="iam">IAM inventory (D11). Christie makes the Console/IAM changes; each one is recorded in the resilience plan</h3>
   275	<table>
   276	<tr><th>Where</th><th>Grant / setting</th><th>Why</th></tr>
   277	<tr><td>Both projects, at creation</td><td>Enforce <code>constraints/iam.automaticIamGrantsForDefaultServiceAccounts</code> (the new project), or remove Editor from the default Compute service account.</td><td>Otherwise the default service account starts with Editor on everything.</td></tr>
   278	<tr><td>Both</td><td>APIs: <code>run</code>, <code>eventarc</code>, <code>cloudfunctions</code>, <code>cloudbuild</code>, <code>artifactregistry</code>, <code>pubsub</code>, <code>cloudscheduler</code>, <code>iamcredentials</code>. Wait for the service agents (<code>gcp-sa-eventarc</code>, the Cloud Functions agent) before the first deploy.</td><td>Needed by 2nd gen. A first deploy that fails while these are propagating isn't a real failure; retry it.</td></tr>
   279	<tr><td>Both</td><td><strong>Amended Sep 29 (M7):</strong> no dedicated build account — the Firebase CLI can't choose one (firebase-functions-deploy-guard K12); Google picks the Compute account or the legacy Cloud Build account. So <strong>both candidates are neutralized</strong> (no Editor, no <code>cloudbuild.builds.builder</code>), and only the account Google actually builds with holds the three build roles: Logs Writer; Artifact Registry Writer on the pre-created <code>gcf-artifacts</code> only; Storage Object Viewer conditioned to the function-source buckets. For <code>my-clay-hub</code> that's the Compute account (D2-1, Sep 29). <em>(Was: a dedicated build service account passed on deploy.)</em></td><td>Otherwise builds run as a default account holding broad roles.</td></tr>
   280	<tr><td>Both</td><td>The deployer (Christie's account, via the guard) holds <code>iam.serviceAccounts.actAs</code> on each <strong>runtime</strong> service account. <strong>Amended Sep 29 (M7):</strong> it comes from Christie's Owner role (recorded in D2-1); if Owner is ever narrowed, a Service Account User grant on each runtime account replaces it. There is no build account to act as.</td><td>Deploying as a chosen service account requires it.</td></tr>
   281	<tr><td><code>tinker-hq-apps</code></td><td><code>clayhub-link@</code>: a custom <strong>read-only</strong> Firestore role (get and list; no create, update or delete), plus <code>roles/eventarc.eventReceiver</code>.</td><td>It reads source docs and receives trigger events.</td></tr>
   282	<tr><td><code>my-clay-hub</code></td><td><code>roles/run.invoker</code> for <code>clayhub-link@tinker-hq-apps</code> on the <strong>Cloud Run service <code>ingestmemberupdate</code></strong> only. The function is declared <code>onRequest({invoker:'private'})</code> (never <code>onCall</code>), with ingress left at "all". Audience = the service's exact <code>run.app</code> URL, called at that same URL, using an OIDC ID token from the metadata server.</td><td>The only way in. Without <code>invoker:'private'</code>, the CLI would grant <code>allUsers</code> (public). Ingress "internal" would block the cross-project call.</td></tr>
   283	<tr><td><code>my-clay-hub</code></td><td><code>ingest@</code>: a custom Firestore role with get, list, create and update, <strong>no delete</strong>.</td><td>Tombstones, never deletes.</td></tr>
   284	<tr><td><code>my-clay-hub</code></td><td><code>auth-admin@</code> (used by <code>claimMembership</code>, <code>exchangeStaffSession</code> and <code>enforceMemberStatus</code>): Firebase Authentication Admin, plus Token Creator on itself.</td><td>Custom claims, custom tokens, revocation, disabling accounts.</td></tr>
   285	<tr><td><code>my-clay-hub</code></td><td>Scheduler identities hold <code>run.invoker</code> on their own scheduled functions (the CLI wires this up; checked, not assumed).</td><td>The 3:30 AM recompute.</td></tr>
   286	<tr><td>Check</td><td>Is either project under a Google Cloud organization with <em>domain-restricted sharing</em>? If it is, confirm the cross-project binding is allowed.</td><td>That policy would block the invoker grant.</td></tr>
   287	</table>
