FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:134:  always carries `schedulerJobList` and `schedulerJobs` (D-4, §9) — for `core`, `schedulerJobs` is `{}` and the list
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:135:  holds every job that is DEPLOYED: each other codebase's scheduled functions as declared at that codebase's newest
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:136:  attempt (e.g. `vault`'s, once `vault` has deployed; a schedule declared but never deployed has no job);
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:137:  `scheduler_attested` is then `n/a` when the list matches, `no` when it doesn't. Only the newest
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:163:A scheduled function (`onSchedule`) is declared in `functions/declarations.json` with `"trigger": "schedule"` and its
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:164:whole schedule — the schedule string, `timeZone` (always `"UTC"`) and all five retry values — and **no** `invoker`: the
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:166:guard refuses a manifest whose schedule or any retry value differs from the declaration, or is unset (the CLI never
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:169:- **The Scheduler job can only be read in the Console.** `functions:list` shows a scheduled function as
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:170:  `scheduleTrigger: {}` and nothing else (J1). So a deploy of a codebase with a schedule **always** needs Christie's
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:171:  reading (`attest_needed` says "the codebase has a schedule"): Cloud Scheduler → region `us-central1` → the **complete**
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:172:  list of job names, and, for each of the codebase's scheduled functions, its job's name
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:173:  (`firebase-schedule-<id>-us-central1`, the id's case kept), state, schedule, time zone, target URL, HTTP method, the
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:176:  exact shape. `--attest` records `scheduler_attested=yes|no|n/a`; `--status` shows it (`unknown (recorded before D-4)`
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:182:  `scheduler_attested` line. A format-1 record (before D-4) without it reads as `unknown (recorded before D-4)`; a
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:185:- **A declaration from before D-4** (exactly `serviceAccount`, `invoker`, `ingress`, no `trigger`) is read as an HTTP
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:188:  after dropping a schedule from its declarations, its job (still in Cloud Scheduler) shows up as an extra job and
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:190:- **Stopping a scheduled function: PAUSE its job** (Cloud Scheduler → the job → Pause). It is immediate and
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:192:  a PAUSED job makes `scheduler_attested=no` until it's resumed and read again. The guard never deletes a function or a
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:197:  - the job kept its **old** schedule or retry settings (the function updated, the job's patch failed): the Scheduler
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:198:    reading shows it (`no`); pause the job, then re-run the same sha;
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:199:  - a schedule **removed from code** leaves its job behind, still calling the function. The deploy itself can't see
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:200:    the job; the next Scheduler reading does (an extra job in the complete list → `scheduler_attested=no`). So before
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:201:    such a change, pause the job, and ask Christie: deleting a job has no guarded path.
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:202:- **The header exception (Christie, Oct 1, O6):** a scheduled function's HTTP response is written by firebase-functions
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:204:  (a public scheduled function would also start a real run). Nothing in the code can make a schedule public: the
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:205:  pinned CLI sets its `run.invoker` to the runtime account (J3), and the probe and the IAM reading check the result.
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:213:- **To stop exports at once:** Cloud Scheduler → `firebase-schedule-vaultExport-us-central1` → **Pause** (§9). Never
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:215:- **A manual catch-up run:** Cloud Scheduler → the job → **Force run** (resume the job first if it's paused). Google
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:216:  sends a Force run the job's *next* scheduled time (seen Oct 7 2026), so `vaultExport` keys a run whose scheduled time
FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md:224:  run's scheduleTime and key (the date); the error names the operation and the prefix. Scheduler retries three times
